A=[
("Which of the following is NOT a muscle of the rotator cuff?","Teres major",["Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Subscapularis"],"The rotator cuff is supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis (SITS)."),
("Which muscle initiates the first 15 degrees of arm abduction?","Supraspinatus",["Deltoid","Trapezius","Serratus anterior"],"Supraspinatus starts abduction, then deltoid takes over. It is supplied by the suprascapular nerve."),
("Which nerve supplies latissimus dorsi?","Thoracodorsal nerve",["Long thoracic nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve"],"The thoracodorsal nerve (C6 to C8) arises from the posterior cord."),
("Which nerve supplies biceps brachii?","Musculocutaneous nerve",["Radial nerve","Median nerve","Axillary nerve"],"The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the flexors of the arm (biceps, brachialis, coracobrachialis)."),
("The first and second lumbricals are supplied by the:","Median nerve",["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve","Axillary nerve"],"The lateral two lumbricals are supplied by the median nerve and the medial two by the ulnar nerve."),
("Which nerve supplies the muscles of the anterior compartment of the thigh?","Femoral nerve",["Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"],"The femoral nerve (L2 to L4) supplies quadriceps, sartorius and pectineus."),
("Adductor magnus has a dual nerve supply from the obturator nerve and the:","Tibial part of the sciatic nerve",["Femoral nerve","Common fibular nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"],"The hamstring part is supplied by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve."),
("The short head of biceps femoris is supplied by the:","Common fibular nerve",["Tibial nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve"],"It is the only hamstring muscle supplied by the common fibular division of the sciatic nerve."),
("Tibialis anterior produces:","Dorsiflexion and inversion of the foot",["Plantarflexion and eversion","Dorsiflexion and eversion","Plantarflexion and inversion"],"It is supplied by the deep fibular nerve."),
("Which structure passes through the greater sciatic foramen above piriformis?","Superior gluteal nerve and vessels",["Sciatic nerve","Pudendal nerve","Inferior gluteal artery"],"All the others pass below piriformis."),
("In the femoral triangle the femoral vein lies:","Medial to the femoral artery",["Lateral to the femoral artery","Anterior to the femoral artery","Lateral to the femoral nerve"],"From lateral to medial: nerve, artery, vein, lymphatics (NAVL)."),
("Which of these is NOT enclosed in the femoral sheath?","Femoral nerve",["Femoral artery","Femoral vein","Femoral canal"],"The femoral nerve lies outside and lateral to the femoral sheath."),
("The adductor canal contains the femoral vessels, the saphenous nerve and the:","Nerve to vastus medialis",["Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve"],"The adductor (subsartorial) canal runs from the apex of the femoral triangle to the adductor hiatus."),
("The popliteal artery divides into its terminal branches at the lower border of:","Popliteus",["Gastrocnemius","Soleus","Plantaris"],"It divides into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries."),
("The dorsalis pedis artery is the continuation of the:","Anterior tibial artery",["Posterior tibial artery","Peroneal artery","Popliteal artery"],"The anterior tibial artery becomes the dorsalis pedis at the ankle."),
("The great saphenous vein passes in front of the:","Medial malleolus",["Lateral malleolus","Tibial tuberosity","Calcaneum"],"It ends in the femoral vein at the saphenous opening."),
("The ankle jerk tests which spinal segments?","S1 and S2",["L3 and L4","L5 and S1","L2 and L3"],"The knee jerk is L3 and L4."),
("The biceps reflex tests which segments?","C5 and C6",["C7 and C8","C3 and C4","T1 and T2"],"The triceps reflex is C7 and C8."),
("Which thenar muscle is supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve?","Abductor pollicis brevis",["Adductor pollicis","First dorsal interosseous","Abductor digiti minimi"],"The recurrent branch supplies abductor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis and the superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis."),
("The dorsal interossei of the hand:","Abduct the fingers",["Adduct the fingers","Flex the interphalangeal joints","Extend the wrist"],"DAB PAD: Dorsal ABduct, Palmar ADduct. Both are supplied by the ulnar nerve."),
("The floor of the anatomical snuffbox is formed by the scaphoid and the:","Trapezium",["Lunate","Capitate","Hamate"],"The radial artery crosses the floor."),
("The cephalic vein drains into the axillary vein after piercing the:","Clavipectoral fascia",["Pectoralis minor","Serratus anterior","Axillary sheath only"],"It runs in the deltopectoral groove."),
("Which vein is usually chosen for venepuncture at the cubital fossa?","Median cubital vein",["Basilic vein only","Cephalic vein only","Brachial vein"],"It lies over the bicipital aponeurosis, which separates it from the brachial artery."),
("From lateral to medial, the cubital fossa contains the biceps tendon, brachial artery and:","Median nerve",["Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"The mnemonic is TAN: tendon, artery, nerve."),
("Which layer of the scalp is known as the danger area?","Loose areolar tissue",["Skin","Dense connective tissue","Pericranium"],"Infection here spreads easily and reaches the cranial cavity through emissary veins."),
("The muscles of mastication are supplied by the:","Mandibular nerve",["Facial nerve","Maxillary nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve"],"The muscles are temporalis, masseter and the medial and lateral pterygoids."),
("The stapedius muscle is supplied by the:","Facial nerve",["Mandibular nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Vagus nerve"],"Tensor tympani is supplied by the mandibular nerve. Paralysis of stapedius causes hyperacusis."),
("The parotid duct pierces which muscle?","Buccinator",["Masseter","Orbicularis oris","Platysma"],"It opens in the cheek opposite the upper second molar."),
("Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue is carried by the:","Chorda tympani",["Lingual nerve proper","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"The chorda tympani (facial nerve) joins the lingual nerve. The lingual nerve carries general sensation."),
("On protrusion, the tongue deviates towards the side of a lesion of the:","Hypoglossal nerve",["Glossopharyngeal nerve","Facial nerve","Lingual nerve"],"Genioglossus on the affected side is weak, so the unopposed healthy side pushes the tongue towards it."),
("Which nerve supplies the superior oblique muscle?","Trochlear nerve",["Oculomotor nerve","Abducens nerve","Ophthalmic nerve"],"LR6 SO4: lateral rectus by the abducens, superior oblique by the trochlear nerve, all the rest by the oculomotor."),
("The efferent limb of the pupillary light reflex is carried by the:","Oculomotor nerve",["Optic nerve","Trochlear nerve","Sympathetic chain"],"Parasympathetic fibres from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus constrict the pupil."),
("Which artery connects the internal carotid artery to the posterior cerebral artery in the circle of Willis?","Posterior communicating artery",["Anterior communicating artery","Basilar artery","Anterior cerebral artery"],"It is the usual site of berry aneurysms with the anterior communicating artery."),
("Lenticulostriate arteries, a common site of hypertensive haemorrhage, branch from the:","Middle cerebral artery",["Anterior cerebral artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Basilar artery"],"They supply the internal capsule and basal ganglia."),
("Broca's area is located in the:","Inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere",["Superior temporal gyrus","Angular gyrus","Precentral gyrus"],"Damage causes non-fluent speech with preserved comprehension."),
("A lesion of the optic chiasm typically produces:","Bitemporal hemianopia",["Homonymous hemianopia","Central scotoma","Binasal hemianopia"],"Crossing nasal fibres are damaged, for example by a pituitary tumour."),
("The apex beat is normally felt in the:","Fifth intercostal space in the midclavicular line",["Fourth intercostal space at the sternal edge","Sixth intercostal space in the anterior axillary line","Second intercostal space on the left"],"The apex is formed by the left ventricle."),
("The coronary sinus opens into the:","Right atrium",["Left atrium","Right ventricle","Superior vena cava"],"It lies in the posterior atrioventricular groove."),
("The ligamentum arteriosum is the remnant of the:","Ductus arteriosus",["Ductus venosus","Umbilical vein","Foramen ovale"],"It connects the pulmonary trunk and the aortic arch."),
("The trachea bifurcates at the level of the:","Sternal angle",["Jugular notch","Xiphisternal joint","Cricoid cartilage"],"This is the T4 to T5 vertebral level."),
("Why are foreign bodies more likely to enter the right main bronchus?","It is wider, shorter and more vertical",["It is longer and narrower","It is angled sharply to the right","It lacks cartilage"],"This also explains aspiration pneumonia in the right lower lobe."),
("The lower border of the pleura in the midaxillary line lies at the level of the:","Tenth rib",["Sixth rib","Eighth rib","Twelfth rib"],"The pleura reaches the 8th rib at the midclavicular line, the 10th at the midaxillary line and the 12th posteriorly."),
("In the costal groove the neurovascular bundle is arranged from above downwards as:","Vein, artery, nerve",["Nerve, artery, vein","Artery, vein, nerve","Artery, nerve, vein"],"A chest drain is therefore inserted just above the upper border of a rib."),
("Which structure passes through the diaphragm at the level of T8?","Inferior vena cava",["Oesophagus","Aorta","Thoracic duct"],"The oesophagus is at T10 and the aorta at T12."),
("The phrenic nerve passes over the anterior surface of:","Scalenus anterior",["Scalenus medius","Sternocleidomastoid","Levator scapulae"],"It arises from C3 to C5."),
("The first constriction of the oesophagus is at the:","Cricopharyngeus (C6)",["Aortic arch","Left bronchus","Diaphragm"],"It lies about 15 cm from the incisors."),
("Which vessel is NOT a branch of the coeliac trunk?","Superior mesenteric artery",["Left gastric artery","Splenic artery","Common hepatic artery"],"The superior mesenteric artery arises separately at L1."),
("The midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two-thirds and distal third of the:","Transverse colon",["Ascending colon","Descending colon","Sigmoid colon"],"The midgut is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery."),
("Which vessel lies behind the neck of the pancreas?","Superior mesenteric vein joining the splenic vein",["Inferior mesenteric artery","Left renal artery","Coeliac trunk"],"The portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas."),
("Carcinoma of the head of the pancreas typically presents with:","Painless obstructive jaundice",["Haematemesis","Dysphagia","Haematuria"],"It compresses the common bile duct as it passes through the head."),
("Calot's triangle is bounded by the cystic duct, the common hepatic duct and the:","Inferior surface of the liver",["Right hepatic artery","Portal vein","Gallbladder fundus"],"The cystic artery usually runs within it, so it is the key area in cholecystectomy."),
("The inferior mesenteric artery arises at the level of:","L3",["T12","L1","L5"],"The coeliac trunk is T12, the SMA L1 and the renal arteries L1 to L2."),
("The left testicular vein drains into the:","Left renal vein",["Inferior vena cava","Left common iliac vein","Left internal iliac vein"],"This is why varicocele is more common on the left."),
("The ureter is crossed by which structure in the female pelvis?","Uterine artery",["Ovarian vein","Inferior epigastric artery","Obturator nerve"],"Water under the bridge: the ureter passes beneath the uterine artery."),
("Internal haemorrhoids arise above the pectinate line and are:","Painless",["Painful","Supplied by somatic nerves","Drained by superficial inguinal nodes"],"Above the line the visceral nerve supply is insensitive to pain."),
("Parasympathetic supply of the pelvic viscera comes from:","S2 to S4",["T10 to L2","L1 to L3","S5 to Co1"],"These are the pelvic splanchnic nerves."),
("The pudendal nerve leaves and re-enters the pelvis around the:","Ischial spine",["Ischial tuberosity","Pubic ramus","Coccyx"],"It passes through the pudendal (Alcock's) canal."),
("The dermatome of the umbilicus is:","T10",["T4","L1","T6"],"The nipple is T4 and the inguinal region L1."),
("In an adult the spinal cord ends at about the level of:","L1 to L2",["L4","S2","T10"],"The dural sac ends at S2."),
("Which layer is NOT crossed by a lumbar puncture needle?","Pia mater",["Ligamentum flavum","Dura mater","Supraspinous ligament"],"The needle ends in the subarachnoid space, which lies outside the pia mater."),
("The artery of Adamkiewicz usually arises at the level of:","T9 to L2",["C3 to C5","T1 to T3","L5 to S1"],"It is the largest radicular artery and supplies the lower spinal cord."),
("In Brown-Sequard syndrome there is contralateral loss of:","Pain and temperature",["Proprioception","Motor power","Vibration sense"],"Spinothalamic fibres decussate soon after entering, so the loss is on the opposite side below the lesion."),
]
